WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Lancaster County and the Amish community. Introduction and location. Lancaster County is in southeastern Pennsylvania.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:21.000
It has a city with museums and restaurants, and also quiet countryside with farms and small towns.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:41.000
Amish country has one of the oldest and largest Amish communities in the United States. The Amish group started in Europe in the 16th century. The first large group came to Lancaster in the early 18th century.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:50.000
They are Christians. People decide to get baptized and join the church when they are adults.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:11.000
The Ordnung is a set of rules for the community. Each church group has different rules for clothes, work and technology.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:20.000
They do not hate modern things. They just use technology carefully. Some people do not use public electricity or own cars, but use other power sources when needed.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:41.000
They speak Pennsylvania Dutch at home. They learn English at school and to talk to visitors. They use Classic German for church and religious reading.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:00.000
You can talk to them in English. Amish people work in farming, woodworking, construction and small shops.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Amish and non-Amish neighbors live and work together in the same area. You can support local businesses, but please respect their private life.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Respect privacy. Always ask before taking a photo of people. Do not enter private homes or farms. Remember that not all Amish families have the exact same rules.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
You can travel to Lancaster County by train, bus or car. Walking or riding a basic bicycle are great options because the land is flat. Public transport does not go to rural roads, so it is best to stay in a town center.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Useful phrases for directions: “Excuse me, how do I get to Lancaster station?” “Follow the signs to the town center.” “Go north on Route 222. Then turn left at the second traffic light. The marker is next to the plaza near the visitor center.”

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:50.000
Slow down when you see a horse carriage. Do not pass a carriage on a sharp curve. Give extra space when you try to pass a carriage. Do not use the horn near horses because it can scare them.